Speckit Constitution

Purpose
- Define the mission, values, governance, and technical rules for the Speckit design/system library used across projects in this repository.

Core Principles
- Predictability: APIs and tokens should behave consistently.
- Accessibility-first: All components must meet WCAG AA baseline.
- Performance: Favor lightweight implementations and hardware-accelerated CSS.
- Evolutionary: Maintain backward-compatible deprecations and clear migration paths.

Scope
- Design tokens: colors, typography, spacing, elevations, motion.
- Component primitives: Button, Input, Select, Grid, Modal, Tooltip, Avatar.
- Utilities: responsive helpers, motion utilities, theme toggling.

Versioning & Releases
- Semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH).
- Breaking changes require a MAJOR bump and documented migration guide.
- Minor releases add backward-compatible features; patches fix bugs.

Governance & Ownership
- Owners: codebase maintainers listed in OWNERS (primary contacts for reviews/releases).
- Decisions: RFC-style proposals stored under docs/rfcs. Consensus via maintainers; fallback: majority of owners.

Contribution Rules
- Open a small focused PR with tests and storybook examples where applicable.
- Include changelog entry for user-visible changes.
- Automated tests and lint must pass before merge.

Design Tokens & Theming
- Tokens are canonical source (tokens/*). Do not hardcode values in components; reference tokens.
- Theme overrides allowed via token maps; prefer extension over mutation.

Accessibility & Testing
- Each interactive component must include keyboard and screen-reader support and accessible examples in stories/tests.
- Add unit and visual regression tests for public-facing components.

Deprecation Policy
- Deprecate with a clear notice, migration example, and a deprecation timeline (minimum one minor release).

Security & Privacy
- Avoid shipping secrets. Report security issues privately to owners.

Enforcement
- CI enforces linting, tests, and token usage rules. Code reviewers enforce the constitution during reviews.

Amendments
- Amendments made via RFC and recorded in this file with dates and authors; maintainers keep the canonical version.

